1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,170
A deploy step fails on the server
with "permission denied" on run.sh.

2
00:00:09,210 --> 00:00:14,358
The same script runs on every laptop in
the team. Nobody changed the server.

3
00:00:14,398 --> 00:00:19,956
Your CTO asks one calm question: is this
a server problem or a repository problem,

4
00:00:19,996 --> 00:00:21,799
and how do you prove which?

5
00:00:22,139 --> 00:00:26,584
You can answer in one command, if you
know what a tree, Git's listing of one

6
00:00:26,624 --> 00:00:30,006
directory, records for each
entry, and what it leaves out.

7
00:00:30,046 --> 00:00:34,963
The answer is in the object store, and by
the end of this video you'll read it there

8
00:00:35,003 --> 00:00:39,625
with plumbing, the low-level commands
with stable output: from a commit, to its

9
00:00:39,665 --> 00:00:44,287
tree, to one entry, to the bytes of one
file. Keep that failing deploy in mind.

10
00:00:44,327 --> 00:00:45,113
It comes back.

11
00:00:46,953 --> 00:00:50,689
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

12
00:00:50,729 --> 00:00:55,764
Last time you opened the .git directory
and recomputed the ID of a loose object by

13
00:00:55,804 --> 00:01:00,530
hand. An object is one unit of Git's
storage, named by a hash of its type and

14
00:01:00,570 --> 00:01:05,481
content, and a hash is a fixed-length
fingerprint. Today: what the four types of

15
00:01:05,521 --> 00:01:10,680
object contain, and the three commands that
read them, git cat-file, git ls-tree and

16
00:01:10,720 --> 00:01:11,237
git show.

17
00:01:11,577 --> 00:01:16,696
All three commands only read. Everything
you see me run in this video carries the 🟢

18
00:01:16,736 --> 00:01:19,617
SAFE label: it reads state
and changes nothing.

19
00:01:19,957 --> 00:01:25,566
We replay two scripts from labs/ch03:
object-types.sh and read-objects.sh.

20
00:01:25,606 --> 00:01:31,903
They use the fixed lab clock, so the object
IDs on my screen are the IDs printed in

21
00:01:31,943 --> 00:01:35,338
Chapter 3 of the book.
Compare them as we go.

22
00:01:37,178 --> 00:01:38,590
After this video you can:

23
00:01:38,930 --> 00:01:42,260
Read the raw form of a tree entry
and list the modes Git uses.

24
00:01:42,600 --> 00:01:46,717
Explain why changing a message three
commits back changes the ID of HEAD.

25
00:01:47,057 --> 00:01:48,827
Read an annotated tag object.

26
00:01:49,167 --> 00:01:52,470
List every object of a repository
with its type and size.

27
00:01:52,810 --> 00:01:56,162
Walk from a commit to the bytes
of one file with plumbing only.

28
00:01:58,002 --> 00:02:03,037
Start with why. Every question about what
Git has stored comes down to four kinds of

29
00:02:03,077 --> 00:02:07,689
object. If you can read each of them in
its raw form, no report from Git is a

30
00:02:07,729 --> 00:02:11,375
mystery, and no claim about
history has to be taken on trust.

31
00:02:11,715 --> 00:02:16,715
In one sentence: a blob is the bytes of one
file, a tree is one directory listing, a

32
00:02:16,755 --> 00:02:21,395
commit is a snapshot with its ancestry
and its metadata, and a tag object is a

33
00:02:21,435 --> 00:02:26,255
named, dated pointer to another object. A
snapshot is the complete state of every

34
00:02:26,295 --> 00:02:27,875
tracked file at one moment.

35
00:02:28,215 --> 00:02:31,875
What each type leaves out matters
as much as what it holds.

36
00:02:31,915 --> 00:02:37,143
A blob holds the bytes of a file, or the
target path of a symbolic link, a file that

37
00:02:37,183 --> 00:02:41,596
only points at another path. It has
no name, no mode and no timestamps.

38
00:02:41,936 --> 00:02:45,998
A tree holds entries of mode, name
and object ID, sorted by name.

39
00:02:46,038 --> 00:02:49,343
It doesn't know its own path
and it holds no history.

40
00:02:49,383 --> 00:02:54,140
A commit holds the headers tree, parent,
author and committer, some optional

41
00:02:54,180 --> 00:02:56,601
headers, a blank line, and the message.

42
00:02:56,941 --> 00:03:02,390
It holds no diff and no branch name. A tag
object holds the headers object, type,

43
00:03:02,430 --> 00:03:07,133
tag and tagger, a blank line, the
message, and optionally a signature.

44
00:03:07,173 --> 00:03:11,944
It gives no guarantee that the ref
of the same name still points at it.

45
00:03:11,984 --> 00:03:14,655
A ref is a name that holds an object ID.

46
00:03:14,995 --> 00:03:19,679
Now how. Each of the four is one entry in
the object database, loose in a file of

47
00:03:19,719 --> 00:03:24,463
its own or packed with others, stored and
named exactly as you saw in the previous

48
00:03:24,503 --> 00:03:27,263
video. The type is the
first word of the header.

49
00:03:27,603 --> 00:03:32,465
Trees first. A tree entry has a
mode, and Git uses five of them.

50
00:03:32,505 --> 00:03:38,900
100644 is a regular file, and the ID names
a blob. 100755 is an executable file, and

51
00:03:38,940 --> 00:03:44,799
the ID names a blob. 120000 is a symbolic
link, and the ID names a blob whose

52
00:03:44,839 --> 00:03:46,867
content is the link target.

53
00:03:47,207 --> 00:03:50,775
040000 is a directory, and
the ID names another tree.

54
00:03:50,815 --> 00:03:56,425
160000 is a gitlink, the record of a
submodule, which is another repository checked

55
00:03:56,465 --> 00:04:01,122
out inside yours, and the ID names
a commit in that other repository.

56
00:04:01,162 --> 00:04:03,368
These five are the complete list.

57
00:04:03,708 --> 00:04:06,836
The modes look like Unix
permissions, and they're not.

58
00:04:06,876 --> 00:04:10,003
The executable bit is the
only permission Git records.

59
00:04:10,043 --> 00:04:14,344
Owner, group, the other bits and every
timestamp aren't in the repository.

60
00:04:14,384 --> 00:04:18,978
A directory exists only as a tree with
entries, which is why an empty directory

61
00:04:19,018 --> 00:04:20,092
can't be committed.

62
00:04:20,432 --> 00:04:25,243
Commits next. A commit has exactly one
tree header: the ID of the top-level tree,

63
00:04:25,283 --> 00:04:29,674
the complete snapshot. It has no parent
header, one, or several, in order.

64
00:04:29,714 --> 00:04:34,643
A parent is a commit this one was built on.
The first parent is the commit you were

65
00:04:34,683 --> 00:04:39,254
on, the second is the one you merged.
On screen is today's demo history: four

66
00:04:39,294 --> 00:04:41,350
commits, the last with two parents.

67
00:04:41,690 --> 00:04:47,387
A commit also has one author and one
committer, each with a name, an email, seconds

68
00:04:47,427 --> 00:04:52,916
since 1 January 1970 UTC, and a time-zone
offset. The committer differs from the

69
00:04:52,956 --> 00:04:57,547
author after an amend, a rebase, a
cherry-pick or an applied patch.

70
00:04:57,587 --> 00:05:02,662
Three headers are optional. encoding
appears when the message isn't UTF-8.

71
00:05:02,702 --> 00:05:08,329
gpgsig is a signature over the rest of the
commit, with that name for OpenPGP, SSH

72
00:05:08,369 --> 00:05:14,135
and X.509 signatures alike. And mergetag is
the complete signed tag object that this

73
00:05:14,175 --> 00:05:15,103
commit merged.

74
00:05:15,443 --> 00:05:20,463
The consequence is the centre of this
video. A commit ID is the hash of the word

75
00:05:20,503 --> 00:05:25,713
commit, the size, a NUL byte, which is a
byte of value zero, and exactly that text.

76
00:05:25,753 --> 00:05:30,647
The text contains the ID of the tree,
which contains the IDs of every blob and

77
00:05:30,687 --> 00:05:31,153
subtree.

78
00:05:31,493 --> 00:05:35,506
It contains the IDs of the parents,
which contain the IDs of theirs.

79
00:05:35,546 --> 00:05:40,512
One commit ID therefore fixes every file in
the snapshot and every commit behind it.

80
00:05:40,552 --> 00:05:45,279
Nothing in a commit can be edited. A
different message, timestamp or parent is a

81
00:05:45,319 --> 00:05:50,107
different text with a different hash.
"Amending" and "rebasing" always create new

82
00:05:50,147 --> 00:05:50,584
commits.

83
00:05:50,924 --> 00:05:55,841
Tags last. A lightweight tag is a ref
that holds a commit ID and nothing else.

84
00:05:55,881 --> 00:06:00,862
An annotated tag is a ref that holds the
ID of a tag object, and the tag object

85
00:06:00,902 --> 00:06:05,184
names the commit, its type, the
tag's own name, who tagged and when.

86
00:06:05,524 --> 00:06:10,321
When do you read objects this way? When
you need proof: of a file mode, of what a

87
00:06:10,361 --> 00:06:13,366
release contains, of what
makes a repository large.

88
00:06:13,406 --> 00:06:18,322
And when not? For everyday reading, git
show and git log format the same data for a

89
00:06:18,362 --> 00:06:20,710
person, and you don't need the raw form.

90
00:06:22,550 --> 00:06:25,638
The textbook's analogy is
a filesystem taken apart.

91
00:06:25,678 --> 00:06:30,667
Blobs are file contents with the name torn
off. Trees are the directory pages that

92
00:06:30,707 --> 00:06:35,696
put names back. A commit is a dated cover
sheet stapled to the top directory page,

93
00:06:35,736 --> 00:06:38,027
noting which cover sheets came before.

94
00:06:38,367 --> 00:06:43,232
Here's where the analogy breaks: at
mutation. A directory entry in a filesystem

95
00:06:43,272 --> 00:06:46,957
points at a place, and the
content of that place can change.

96
00:06:46,997 --> 00:06:52,110
A tree entry points at content. Change one
file, and its blob, every tree above it,

97
00:06:52,150 --> 00:06:54,842
and the commit are new
objects with new IDs.

98
00:06:55,182 --> 00:06:58,393
Hold that in mind for the
interview question at the end.

99
00:06:58,433 --> 00:07:02,980
A change anywhere propagates upward through
the trees and forward through every

100
00:07:03,020 --> 00:07:07,102
descendant commit, because each
object's ID covers the IDs it contains.

101
00:07:08,942 --> 00:07:15,404
Start on the left with the ref refs/tags/v1.0.0.
It's a name, and it holds one ID:

102
00:07:15,444 --> 00:07:20,478
the tag object 0b624ed. The tag
object points at commit 0c2cf43.

103
00:07:20,518 --> 00:07:26,821
That commit has one arrow marked "tree",
to tree 31fc0d3, and the six entries of

104
00:07:26,861 --> 00:07:30,865
that tree are listed on the
right with their modes.

105
00:07:30,905 --> 00:07:37,049
Then two parent arrows go down: the first
parent 4f2cc0c and the second parent

106
00:07:37,089 --> 00:07:41,172
62001eb, which both lead to
the root commit b602c1f.

107
00:07:41,512 --> 00:07:47,169
Look at the last tree entry. Mode 160000,
type commit, ID 9eb542d, and the note "in

108
00:07:47,209 --> 00:07:51,219
another repository". That commit
is not stored here at all.

109
00:07:51,559 --> 00:07:55,452
Every arrow is an object ID stored
inside the object at its tail.

110
00:07:55,492 --> 00:08:00,414
There are none in the other direction. A
blob doesn't know which trees contain it.

111
00:08:00,454 --> 00:08:03,500
A commit doesn't know its
children or its branches.

112
00:08:05,340 --> 00:08:10,204
The demo repository is a small inference
service. Its top-level directory contains

113
00:08:10,244 --> 00:08:12,297
every kind of entry Git can record.

114
00:08:12,637 --> 00:08:17,468
Predict before I show it. Six entries in
the working tree, the files you edit.

115
00:08:17,508 --> 00:08:22,215
How many different modes will git ls-tree
HEAD print, and what type will the

116
00:08:22,255 --> 00:08:25,524
tokenizer line show? HEAD
means the commit you're on.

117
00:08:25,564 --> 00:08:26,149
I'll wait.

118
00:08:30,989 --> 00:08:36,969
Five modes. Look at run.sh: mode
100755. That's the answer to the hook.

119
00:08:37,009 --> 00:08:43,923
If this line said 100644, the executable
bit was never recorded, and the server is

120
00:08:43,963 --> 00:08:50,028
innocent. Now current-model: mode
120000, and its blob contains the text

121
00:08:50,068 --> 00:08:56,133
models/v2.bin, the link target. And
tokenizer: mode 160000, type commit.

122
00:08:56,173 --> 00:08:59,610
If you said blob, you're in good company.

123
00:08:59,950 --> 00:09:01,584
Now the stored form of a tree.

124
00:09:01,924 --> 00:09:07,005
Read the hex dump, the raw bytes written as
hexadecimal digits, against the listing.

125
00:09:07,045 --> 00:09:11,699
Each entry is the mode in ASCII, here
40000 without the leading zero that the

126
00:09:11,739 --> 00:09:16,758
listing prints, then a space, the name, a
NUL byte, and the object ID as twenty raw

127
00:09:16,798 --> 00:09:19,867
bytes. The type column of
the listing isn't stored.

128
00:09:19,907 --> 00:09:21,635
Git derives it from the mode.

129
00:09:21,975 --> 00:09:22,498
Commits.

130
00:09:22,838 --> 00:09:26,110
HEAD is a merge, a commit
with more than one parent.

131
00:09:26,150 --> 00:09:31,460
How many tree headers and how many parent
headers? And HEAD~2 is the first commit of

132
00:09:31,500 --> 00:09:35,091
the repository: how many parent
headers there? I'll wait.

133
00:09:39,931 --> 00:09:45,348
One tree, two parents for the merge. No
parent header at all for the root commit,

134
00:09:45,388 --> 00:09:49,660
the first one. Notice what isn't
there: no diff, no branch name.

135
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:55,657
Try it now, thirty seconds, in a repository
in the lab shell: run git cat-file -p

136
00:09:55,697 --> 00:09:58,681
HEAD and count the
parent lines. I'll wait.

137
00:10:03,521 --> 00:10:08,822
One parent line for an ordinary commit,
several for a merge, none for the very first

138
00:10:08,862 --> 00:10:09,267
commit.

139
00:10:09,607 --> 00:10:14,745
The two IDs are equal. The commit ID is the
hash of the header and exactly the text

140
00:10:14,785 --> 00:10:17,490
you read a moment ago.
Nothing else goes in.

141
00:10:17,830 --> 00:10:23,529
Same text in, same ID out: 0c2cf43,
from shasum and from Git alike.

142
00:10:23,869 --> 00:10:24,925
The annotated tag.

143
00:10:25,265 --> 00:10:28,740
One of these two tags is
annotated and one is lightweight.

144
00:10:28,780 --> 00:10:31,649
What type will each one
report? Say it out loud.

145
00:10:36,489 --> 00:10:43,668
v1.0.0 is type tag. v1.0.0-rc1 is type
commit, because a lightweight tag is a ref

146
00:10:43,708 --> 00:10:50,976
that holds a commit ID. In the last command,
the first line is the tag object, and

147
00:10:51,016 --> 00:10:58,373
v1.0.0^{} peels it: it follows the tag until
it reaches something that isn't a tag.

148
00:10:58,413 --> 00:11:00,690
The peeled ID equals HEAD.

149
00:11:01,030 --> 00:11:03,774
One optional header,
because it's deterministic.

150
00:11:04,114 --> 00:11:08,784
This is the one state-changing command of
the video: git commit adds a commit and

151
00:11:08,824 --> 00:11:11,633
moves the branch. The demo
makes it in a sandbox.

152
00:11:11,973 --> 00:11:15,777
Look at the encoding line between
the committer and the blank line.

153
00:11:15,817 --> 00:11:20,309
The gpgsig and mergetag headers aren't
printed in the book, because a signature

154
00:11:20,349 --> 00:11:21,514
differs on every run.

155
00:11:24,354 --> 00:11:29,003
Three names, three types. -s gives the
size of the content without the header.

156
00:11:29,043 --> 00:11:33,992
-e prints nothing: it answers with the exit
status only, the number a command hands

157
00:11:34,032 --> 00:11:38,260
back when it ends, zero when the
object exists and one when it doesn't.

158
00:11:38,600 --> 00:11:41,852
Starting one process per
object is slow in a script.

159
00:11:41,892 --> 00:11:46,474
The batch modes read names from standard
input and answer in one process.

160
00:11:46,814 --> 00:11:50,938
A quick quiz. Five names go in,
and one of them doesn't exist.

161
00:11:50,978 --> 00:11:54,833
Does the command stop there,
or carry on? Say it out loud.

162
00:11:59,673 --> 00:12:03,184
It carries on: the line reads
no-such-branch missing.

163
00:12:03,224 --> 00:12:08,746
The default line is ID, type, size. In the
second command, %(rest) repeats whatever

164
00:12:08,786 --> 00:12:14,040
followed the name on the input line, which
is how each path reaches the output.

165
00:12:14,080 --> 00:12:19,133
The last line is the gitlink: the tree
names commit 9eb542d, and this object

166
00:12:19,173 --> 00:12:22,819
database doesn't contain it.
"Missing" here is correct.

167
00:12:23,159 --> 00:12:28,143
Twenty-two objects for four commits.
--batch-all-objects visits loose and packed

168
00:12:28,183 --> 00:12:33,231
objects without walking any history, so
it also finds objects that nothing refers

169
00:12:33,271 --> 00:12:38,255
to. The second pipeline is the first one
to run when a repository is larger than

170
00:12:38,295 --> 00:12:38,820
expected.

171
00:12:42,160 --> 00:12:46,195
Without -r you see one directory
level, with trees as entries.

172
00:12:46,235 --> 00:12:51,453
With -r you see only the leaves: the flat
list of paths a checkout would create.

173
00:12:51,493 --> 00:12:56,646
-t adds the trees passed on the way. -l
prints sizes, and a dash where an entry

174
00:12:56,686 --> 00:13:00,787
isn't a blob. -d keeps the
directories, the gitlink among them.

175
00:13:07,127 --> 00:13:10,725
git show is the porcelain on
top, the user-facing layer.

176
00:13:10,765 --> 00:13:16,118
It picks a presentation by object type: the
content of a blob, the names in a tree,

177
00:13:16,158 --> 00:13:21,576
the tag object followed by its commit, and
with --format=raw the headers of a commit

178
00:13:21,616 --> 00:13:22,226
as stored.

179
00:13:24,066 --> 00:13:25,714
Five mistakes to watch for.

180
00:13:26,054 --> 00:13:31,184
Reading a mode as Unix permissions. Root
cause: Git records only the executable bit;

181
00:13:31,224 --> 00:13:35,492
owner, group, the other bits and
timestamps are not in the repository.

182
00:13:35,832 --> 00:13:40,436
Treating "missing" for a gitlink as
corruption. Root cause: the commit a gitlink

183
00:13:40,476 --> 00:13:45,022
names lives in another repository, so
this object database does not contain it.

184
00:13:45,362 --> 00:13:50,224
Believing a commit can be edited in place.
Root cause: the commit ID is the hash of

185
00:13:50,264 --> 00:13:55,067
the commit's text, so any change produces
a different object, and every descendant

186
00:13:55,107 --> 00:13:56,012
changes with it.

187
00:13:56,352 --> 00:13:59,957
Assuming an annotated tag
ref points at the commit.

188
00:13:59,997 --> 00:14:05,745
Root cause: the ref holds the ID of the
tag object; you peel it with ^{} to reach

189
00:14:05,785 --> 00:14:06,531
the commit.

190
00:14:06,871 --> 00:14:12,033
Trying to commit an empty directory. Root
cause: a directory exists only as a tree

191
00:14:12,073 --> 00:14:12,857
with entries.

192
00:14:14,697 --> 00:14:20,113
Now, out of the lab, and back to the deploy
that fails with "permission denied" on

193
00:14:20,153 --> 00:14:25,369
run.sh. On the build machine you run git
ls-tree HEAD run.sh and read the mode.

194
00:14:25,409 --> 00:14:30,625
It shows 100644. The executable bit was
never recorded, so every fresh checkout

195
00:14:30,665 --> 00:14:35,947
creates the file without it. The laptops
work only because their files were made

196
00:14:35,987 --> 00:14:41,469
executable by hand. So the CTO has an answer:
a repository problem, proved with one

197
00:14:41,509 --> 00:14:46,459
command. The fix is a commit that changes
the mode, which Chapter 4 covers.

198
00:14:46,799 --> 00:14:52,736
A second case from the same team. The
release notes say a new release is "the same

199
00:14:52,776 --> 00:14:58,787
code" as the last one. You compare git
rev-parse v1.0.0^{tree} with the tree of the

200
00:14:58,827 --> 00:15:03,379
other tag. Equal tree IDs mean
identical content in every file.

201
00:15:03,419 --> 00:15:09,284
And in CI, before deploying a commit ID
that arrived as a parameter, the pipeline

202
00:15:09,324 --> 00:15:15,334
runs git cat-file -e "$id^{commit}", which
fails for a missing object and for an ID

203
00:15:15,374 --> 00:15:18,250
that names anything other than a commit.

204
00:15:20,090 --> 00:15:25,606
Your turn. Do Exercise 16.2, "From a
commit to the bytes of a file", in

205
00:15:25,646 --> 00:15:30,928
exercises/m16-m18-internals.md. You
walk from HEAD to the content of

206
00:15:30,968 --> 00:15:36,249
config/shards.yaml with git cat-file,
git rev-parse and git ls-tree.

207
00:15:36,589 --> 00:15:41,188
Before you type anything, write down your
predictions: how many objects you'll read

208
00:15:41,228 --> 00:15:45,826
on the way, the type of each, and which
mode a subdirectory, an executable file and

209
00:15:45,866 --> 00:15:49,459
a submodule would have. Then run
the commands and check yourself.

210
00:15:49,799 --> 00:15:54,198
The challenge is Exercise 16.4, "Count
the objects", in the same file.

211
00:15:54,238 --> 00:15:57,179
Predict the counts per
type before you ask Git.

212
00:15:59,019 --> 00:16:01,736
Question 72 of the CTO question bank:

213
00:16:02,076 --> 00:16:05,958
A strong answer starts from what
is hashed: the commit's own text.

214
00:16:05,998 --> 00:16:10,832
It then names what that text contains, the
tree ID and the parent IDs, and follows

215
00:16:10,872 --> 00:16:15,765
the chain in both directions: down into
every file of the snapshot and back through

216
00:16:15,805 --> 00:16:20,757
every ancestor. It ends with the practical
consequence for rewriting history and for

217
00:16:20,797 --> 00:16:24,620
trust in an ID. Say it out loud
before you open the answers file.

218
00:16:30,960 --> 00:16:35,786
Let's land this. One commit ID fixes
every file and every commit behind it.

219
00:16:36,126 --> 00:16:37,786
You should now be able to say:

220
00:16:38,126 --> 00:16:43,654
A blob has content and no name; a tree gives
names and modes to IDs; a commit names

221
00:16:43,694 --> 00:16:47,679
one tree and its parents; a tag
object names another object.

222
00:16:48,019 --> 00:16:52,552
Git uses five tree entry modes, and the
executable bit is the only permission it

223
00:16:52,592 --> 00:16:53,010
records.

224
00:16:53,350 --> 00:16:58,302
A commit ID covers its tree and its parents,
so one ID fixes every file and every

225
00:16:58,342 --> 00:16:59,350
commit behind it.

226
00:16:59,690 --> 00:17:05,136
git cat-file answers type, size, content
and existence, for one object or for all of

227
00:17:05,176 --> 00:17:06,442
them in one process.

228
00:17:06,782 --> 00:17:11,073
git ls-tree lists a tree; git show
formats any object for a person.

229
00:17:12,913 --> 00:17:16,522
Read sections 3.4 and 3.5 of Chapter 3.

230
00:17:16,862 --> 00:17:20,158
Today you read all four object
types in their raw form.

231
00:17:20,198 --> 00:17:24,950
Practise that before the next video:
turning names into IDs with git rev-parse.

232
00:17:24,990 --> 00:17:28,105
Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

233
00:17:28,145 --> 00:17:29,560
See you in the next one.
